{
  "summary": "Onboarding activation fell from 58.2% to 51.2% (-7.1 points), and the segment and channel checks attribute most of the change to shifts in who signed up rather than lower activation within groups (s1, s2). The evidence supports a mix shift as the leading explanation, but does not establish why the mix changed or prove a product cause (s1, s2, s5, s6).",
  "observed_changes": [
    {
      "text": "Activation fell from 58.2% in 2025-07-01–2025-09-30 to 51.2% in 2025-10-01–2025-10-31 (-7.1 points) (s1).",
      "evidence": ["s1"]
    },
    {
      "text": "By segment, the mix change contributed -9.2 points while within-segment rate changes contributed +2.1 points; Individual rose from 6% to 41% of the segment mix (s1).",
      "evidence": ["s1"]
    },
    {
      "text": "By channel, the mix change contributed -22.7 points while within-channel rate changes contributed +1.1 points; paid_campaign rose from 0% to 38% of the channel mix (s2).",
      "evidence": ["s2"]
    },
    {
      "text": "Invite acceptance rose from 63.2% to 64.4%, rather than falling alongside activation (s3).",
      "evidence": ["s3"]
    }
  ],
  "possible_explanations": [
    {
      "text": "A shift in segment and channel composition is the leading explanation for the lower overall activation rate; the data does not establish what drove those mix shifts.",
      "type": "segment_mix",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "confidence_reason": "Both activation decompositions and the mostly_mix checks point to mix rather than within-group deterioration. Confidence is not high because the underlying reason for the mix shift is unknown and several channel groups have small samples (s1, s2).",
      "evidence": ["s1", "s2"],
      "conflicting": "Within-group rate effects were positive in both decompositions, which argues against a broad decline in activation behavior; this does not contradict mix being the main explanation for the overall drop (s1, s2)."
    },
    {
      "text": "A product change could have contributed indirectly, but the release evidence provides timing only and does not establish an effect on activation.",
      "type": "product_change",
      "confidence": "low",
      "confidence_reason": "A release is listed near the period, but there is no evidence connecting it to activation, and the mix checks point elsewhere (s1, s2, s5).",
      "evidence": ["s5"],
      "conflicting": "The release result explicitly cautions that timing is not evidence of cause; no feedback records were found to support this explanation (s5, s6)."
    }
  ],
  "conflicting_evidence": [
    {
      "text": "The net within-group activation contribution was positive, and invite acceptance also increased; these findings weaken an explanation based on a general deterioration in onboarding behavior (s1, s2, s3).",
      "evidence": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
    },
    {
      "text": "No customer feedback records mentioning activation, onboarding, or invites were found in the searched Onboarding feedback (s6).",
      "evidence": ["s6"]
    }
  ],
  "ruled_out": [
    {
      "text": "A decline in invite acceptance is not supported: the measured invite acceptance rate increased over the comparison periods (s3).",
      "evidence": ["s3"]
    },
    {
      "text": "A broad within-group activation decline is not supported by the decomposition, which shows a positive net within-group contribution (s1, s2).",
      "evidence": ["s1", "s2"]
    }
  ],
  "data_quality": [
    {
      "text": "No low-volume days were flagged in the target window, but this check alone does not verify that all tracking was complete (s4).",
      "evidence": ["s4"]
    },
    {
      "text": "Channel rates for organic, paid_campaign, partner, and sales are noisy because those groups had fewer than 30 records in a period; interpret channel-level rates cautiously (s2).",
      "evidence": ["s2"]
    }
  ],
  "suggested_experiments": [
    "Break down activation over time by segment and channel, and verify whether the mix shift persists when comparing like-for-like signup cohorts (s1, s2).",
    "Check acquisition and signup records for why paid_campaign and Individual shares changed, and validate the associated channel and segment classifications (s1, s2).",
    "Review exposure to release-08 and compare activation for exposed and unexposed users, controlling for segment and channel mix; treat this as a test, not an assumed cause (s1, s2, s5)."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "The results identify mix shifts but do not explain what caused them (s1, s2).",
    "Small samples make several channel-level rates noisy, and the feedback search returned no records (s2, s6)."
  ]
}
